/-! # Finite-Tor internal Hom over an arbitrary field

Source: Bhatt--Scholze, The pro-etale topology for schemes, Lemma 6.7.13,
Definition 6.5.1 and its finite-coefficient comparison paragraph
(blueprint: lem-per-finite-tor-internal-hom-any-field).

The operation is the ambient ringed-site derived internal Hom over the constant ring
sheaf. Both inputs have finite Tor amplitude. The output has intrinsically bounded
constructible cohomology, including finite stalks, and bounded constructible etale origin.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory Opposite

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme

/-- Internal Hom of bounded constructible finite-Tor complexes over a separated
finite-type scheme over any field is bounded constructible and has bounded constructible
etale origin. No perfection, positive characteristic or separable closedness is imposed.
Source: Bhatt--Scholze, Lemma 6.7.13 and Definition 6.5.1, pp. 49 and 57
(blueprint: lem-per-finite-tor-internal-hom-any-field).

Proof plan (the DAG node's steps):
1. The coefficient facts [lem-weil-l-adic-extension-integers-dvr] and
[lem-weil-valued-dvr-finite-quotients] make $A=R/\varpi^n$ a finite commutative ring. It is
Noetherian: every ideal is generated by its finitely many elements. Moreover $\ell\in\mathfrak
m_R$ because its extended absolute value is $\ell^{-1}<1$. Write $\ell=u\varpi^e$ by clause (5)
of the first node; $e\geq1$, since $u$ is a unit and $\ell$ is in the maximal ideal. Thus
$\ell^n=0$ in $A$. The integer $\ell^n$ is invertible in $k$, as required for finite-coefficient
direct-image finiteness. For a scalar $A$-module, finite as a set and finitely presented are
equivalent: a finite module has a finite free surjection whose kernel is finite and hence
finitely generated; a finite presentation has finite underlying cokernel.

2. A field is Noetherian (its ideals are $0$ and $k$), so
[lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-noetherian] makes every finite-type $k$-scheme below Noetherian.
Such a scheme is quasi-separated by [lem-weil-pbc-locally-noetherian-quasi-separated]. Its open
subschemes are Noetherian and quasi-compact by
[lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-open-subscheme-noetherian]. Each immersion is locally of finite type by
[lem-weil-pbc-immersion-base-change-lft](2); its source is quasi-compact and its target
quasi-separated, so it is quasi-compact by [lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-morphism-permanence](1), hence
finite type. Composing finite-type morphisms is checked on affine charts by adjoining both
finite sets of algebra generators. Consequently all locally closed strata, their
scheme-theoretic closures, and the open complements used below are finite type over $k$ and
Noetherian. The same permanence node makes morphisms between them qcqs. These are the geometric
hypotheses needed below, without an assumption on the perfection or cohomological dimension of
$k$.

3. For such a scheme $T$, write $\mathcal B(T)$ for the ambient $A_T$-complexes with bounded
cohomology and a finite constructible locally closed stratification for every cohomology sheaf,
with finite locally constant module values. Step 1 identifies finite values with finitely
presented values. Thus [lem-sfb-noetherian-global-constructibility] identifies this global
convention with the affine-local convention in
[lem-sfb-discrete-noetherian-constructible-serre]. The latter supplies zero, kernels, cokernels
and extensions in the sheaf category. Apply [lem-sbc-cohomology-in-weak-serre-subcategory] to
conclude that $\mathcal B(T)$ is closed under isomorphisms, shifts and distinguished triangles.
It is also closed under retracts: if $C\xrightarrow{s}D\xrightarrow{r}C$ has $rs=1$, then
$H^q(C)$ is isomorphic to $\ker(1-H^q(s)H^q(r))$ on $H^q(D)$. Kernel closure gives
constructibility, and the retraction gives vanishing whenever $H^q(D)=0$. This verifies
precisely the thick closure required for the generation argument.

4. Ordinary inverse image $v^*$ for a morphism of these schemes preserves $\mathcal B$. With
constant coefficients the pulled-back ring is the same constant ring, so the underlying functor
is exact by [lem-weil-pbc-site-pullback-abelian-exact]. By
[lem-weil-pbc-exact-functor-complex-cohomology], $H^q(v^*C)=v^*H^q(C)$, preserving the
cohomological interval. Pull back a witnessing partition by actual fibre products of its locally
closed subschemes. For a stratum $\iota:Z\to T$ with base change $\iota':Z'=Z\times_TT'\to T'$
and $v':Z'\to Z$, one has $\iota'^*v^*\cong v'^*\iota^*$, since $v\iota'=\iota v'$ and inverse
images are left adjoints of the strictly composing direct images. By
[lem-exc-proetale-pullback-locally-constant] applied to $v'$, the base-changed trivializing
covers trivialize $v'^*\iota^*H^q(C)$ with the identical finite module models. These strata are
again a finite disjoint constructible locally closed partition, and the constant local modules
are unchanged. Hence its cohomology remains constructible with finite values. This applies in
particular to open and closed inverse images. The actual stratification pullback is
[lemma-constructible-pullback-stratification]. For constructibility of its subsets use
[lem-weil-pbc-constructible-preimage]: every open in the Noetherian source is retrocompact,
since its intersection with a quasi-compact open is again an open of a Noetherian space and
hence quasi-compact. Thus its inverse-image hypothesis holds.

5. Ordinary pro-etale $Rf_*$ preserves $\mathcal B$ for every finite-type morphism $f:T\to V$
between these schemes. Choose a bounded constructible etale origin $C_0$ for $C\in\mathcal B(T)$
by [lem-weil-pbc-finite-ring-constructible-derived-origin]. Step 1 gives an annihilating integer
$\ell^n$ invertible in $k$, so [thm-dg-finite-type-constructibility] gives
$Rf_{\mathrm{et},*}C_0\in D^b_c(V_{\mathrm{et}},A)$. Since $f$ is qcqs by step 2 and $C_0$ is
bounded below, [sk-thm-pe-direct-image-comparison] gives
$$Rf_{\mathrm{proet},*}C\cong\nu_V^*Rf_{\mathrm{et},*}C_0.$$ The first cited node preserves
cohomological intervals and identifies bounded constructible etale origin with intrinsic bounded
constructibility, so this belongs to $\mathcal B(V)$. This invokes a sheaf-valued direct-image
theorem, with no bound on the absolute Galois cohomological dimension of $k$.

6. For a closed immersion $g:Z\hookrightarrow T$ with open complement $h:W\hookrightarrow T$,
and $N\in\mathcal B(T)$, use [lem-exc-proetale-support-triangle]: $$g_*Rg^!N\longrightarrow
N\longrightarrow Rh_*h^*N\longrightarrow g_*Rg^!N[1].$$ Its hypotheses are exactly that $T$ is
Noetherian and $A$ finite. By steps 4 and 5, $Rh_*h^*N\in\mathcal B(T)$; step 3 and the triangle
put $g_*Rg^!N$ there too. The complementary open is quasi-compact by step 2. The exactness of
$g_*$ and the identity $g^*g_*\cong1$ are stated in [lem-weil-pbc-proetale-ringed-open-closed].
Apply the exact, constructible-preserving $g^*$ of step 4 to obtain $Rg^!N\in\mathcal B(Z)$. No
constructible-preservation conclusion was assumed in the ambient support-triangle node.

7. Let $L$ be a locally constant sheaf with finite projective $A$-values on a stratum $S$, and
$N\in\mathcal B(S)$. Then $L$ is site-perfect in degree zero, and
[lem-sfb-ringed-site-perfect-duality] gives $$R\mathcal Hom_{A_S}(L,N)\cong
L^\vee\otimes^L_{A_S}N.$$ Here $L^\vee$ is also a locally constant finite projective sheaf in
degree zero: on a trivializing cover its value is $\operatorname{Hom}_A(P,A)$ for the finite
projective scalar value $P$ of $L$. Explicitly write $P$ as the image of an idempotent $e$ on
$A^r$; its dual is the image of the transposed idempotent on $A^r$. Tensoring with it is a
direct summand of $r$ copies of the identity and is exact. Thus the displayed complex has
$H^q=L^\vee\otimes H^q(N)$ and vanishes outside the same interval as $N$, independently of $r$.
To see constructibility, use the finite stratification witnessing constructibility of $H^q(N)$,
then restrict $L^\vee$ and refine its local trivializing cover with that of $H^q(N)$. The
resulting module value is $P^\vee\otimes_A Q$, a finite module. Restriction of the displayed
derived Hom to these site covers is justified by
[lem-weil-pbc-ringed-internal-hom-exact-restriction]. Hence the original finite stratification
witnesses constructibility of each cohomology sheaf, so $R\mathcal Hom(L,N)\in\mathcal B(S)$.

8. The hypothesis on $K$ means exactly that it lies in the finite-Tor bounded constructible
subcategory of [det-sf-flat-model-generation], with the ordinary constant coefficient sheaf and
ambient derived category used here. Its clause (3), valid for every Noetherian scheme and finite
commutative ring on the pro-etale site, says that $K$ is generated by $a_!L$ under shifts,
cones, isomorphisms and direct summands, where $a:S\hookrightarrow X$ is locally closed and $L$
is locally constant with finite projective $A$-values. Use that node's factorization convention
$S\xrightarrow{j}Z\xrightarrow{g}X$, with $j$ open in the scheme-theoretic closure and $g$
closed, and $a_!L=g_*j_!L$. These schemes and open complements satisfy step 2. This is a
generation argument for $K$ itself; it does not assert that the cohomology sheaves of a
finite-Tor complex have finite Tor dimension.

9. For a generator of step 8, apply [lem-weil-stratum-ambient-hom] separately to $g$ and to $j$,
in exactly this order. Its pro-etale hypothesis is Noetherianity, established in step 2. For the
closed case take its open factor to be the identity; for the open case take its closed factor to
be the identity, so the exceptional inverse image is ordinary $j^*$. Since $g_*$ is exact by
[lem-weil-pbc-proetale-ringed-open-closed], the resulting formulas are $$\begin{aligned}
R\mathcal Hom_{A_X}(g_*j_!L,M)&\cong Rg_*R\mathcal Hom_{A_Z}(j_!L,Rg^!M)\\ &\cong
Rg_*Rj_*R\mathcal Hom_{A_S}(L,j^*Rg^!M). \end{aligned}$$ These are ambient internal sheaf Hom
identities, without preservation hypotheses. Step 6 gives $Rg^!M\in\mathcal B(Z)$, step 4 gives
$j^*Rg^!M\in\mathcal B(S)$, and step 7 puts the innermost Hom in $\mathcal B(S)$. Step 5 applied
first to $j$ and then $g$ puts the whole expression in $\mathcal B(X)$. The factorization has
not been interchanged, so no extra factorization-comparison assertion is needed.

10. Fix $M$. Let $\mathcal C$ be the full subcategory of ambient complexes $P$ for which
$R\mathcal Hom(P,M)\in\mathcal B(X)$. The triangle and shift clauses of
[lem-weil-pbc-ringed-internal-hom-exact-restriction], together with step 3, make $\mathcal C$
closed under shifts, cones and isomorphisms. It contains zero. It is closed under retracts: if
$P$ is a retract of $Q$, contravariance gives $R\mathcal Hom(P,M)$ as a retract of $R\mathcal
Hom(Q,M)$, and use step 3. Step 9 puts all the generators of step 8 in $\mathcal C$. The
smallest-subcategory assertion of [det-sf-flat-model-generation](3) therefore puts $K$ in
$\mathcal C$. Consequently $R\mathcal Hom_{A_X}(K,M)$ has bounded constructible cohomology with
finite stalks. In fact this argument only used the bounded constructibility of $M$; the
additional finite-Tor hypothesis on $M$ is harmless and has not been removed from the statement.

11. Apply [lem-weil-pbc-finite-ring-constructible-derived-origin] to the resulting object on $X$
to obtain its bounded constructible etale origin. This gives every clause of the statement in
the intrinsic finite-torsion convention. For the empty scheme or either zero input all objects
in the construction are zero. The proof uses no characteristic restriction beyond invertibility
of $\ell$, and no perfectness or separable-closedness of $k$.
-/
theorem finiteTor_internalHom_constructible_anyField
    (k : Type u) [Field k] (l : ℕ) [Fact l.Prime] (_hl : IsUnit (l : k))
    (E : Type u) [Field E] [Algebra (Padic l) E] [FiniteDimensional (Padic l) E]
    (n : ℕ) (hn : 1 ≤ n) (X : Scheme.{u}) [X.Over (Spec (.of k))]
    [IsSeparated (X ↘ Spec (.of k))] [LocallyOfFiniteType (X ↘ Spec (.of k))]
    [QuasiCompact (X ↘ Spec (.of k))]
    (K M : proEtaleDerivedCategory X (Padic.coefficientExtensionRing l E n))
    (_hK : WeilConjectures.finiteTorConstructible l E n hn X K)
    (_hM : WeilConjectures.finiteTorConstructible l E n hn X M) :
    intrinsicFiniteTorsionConstructible l E n hn X
      ((derivedInternalHomOfUniverses.{u + 1, u, u}
        (ProEt.topology X) (proEtaleConstantRing X (Padic.coefficientExtensionRing l E n))).obj
          (op K, M)) ∧
    ∃ L : Etale.DerivedCategoryModule
        (ULift.{u + 1, u} (Padic.coefficientExtensionRing l E n)) X,
      @Etale.DerivedCategory.isBoundedConstructible
        (ULift.{u + 1, u} (Padic.coefficientExtensionRing l E n)) _
        (@Finite.of_equiv _ _ (Padic.finite_coefficientExtensionRing l E n hn)
          Equiv.ulift.symm) X L ∧
      Nonempty
        (((@ProEtale.finiteCoefficientIntrinsicDerivedComparison X
            (Padic.coefficientExtensionRing l E n) _
            (Padic.finite_coefficientExtensionRing l E n hn)).obj L) ≅
          (derivedInternalHomOfUniverses.{u + 1, u, u}
            (ProEt.topology X)
            (proEtaleConstantRing X (Padic.coefficientExtensionRing l E n))).obj (op K, M)) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme
